  "body": "## Finding description and impact\n\nRoot Cause: validatePrice() reconstructs a “cumulative price” using getPrice() and elapsedTime, forcing timeWeightedAverage == tradePrice and neutralizing the slippage check.\n\nToy example:\n\nIn block N, an MEV actor manipulates the Uniswap V2 pool reserves and ends the block with an off-market spot price.\nIn block N+1, the actor calls BuyBackBurner.buyBack() using protocol-held secondToken.\nUniswapPriceOracle.validatePrice() returns true because the computed “TWAP” equals the current spot price.\nBuyBackBurnerUniswap._performSwap() executes with amountOutMin = 0, allowing execution at the manipulated price.\nLocation: UniswapPriceOracle.sol#L53-L91\n\nBuyBackBurner supports permissionless V2 buybacks of OLAS using protocol-held funds via buyBack(address,uint256). Before swapping, _buyOLAS() enforces slippage protection through IOracle(poolOracle).validatePrice(maxSlippage). The V2 swap itself is executed with amountOutMin = 0 in BuyBackBurnerUniswap._performSwap().\n\nHowever, UniswapPriceOracle.validatePrice() does not compute a TWAP. It reads cumulativePriceLast and blockTimestampLast from the pair, then sets tradePrice = getPrice(), computes cumulativePrice = cumulativePriceLast + tradePrice * elapsedTime, and finally computes timeWeightedAverage = (cumulativePrice - cumulativePriceLast) / elapsedTime (validatePrice()). This algebraically forces timeWeightedAverage == tradePrice whenever elapsedTime > 0, which makes derivation == 0 and causes validatePrice() to accept any spot price. The only exception is the same-timestamp guard (block.timestamp == blockTimestampLast) where the function returns false, which does not provide multi-block manipulation resistance. As a result, price manipulation around buyBack() is not meaningfully constrained by the intended oracle check, and swaps can execute at materially unfavorable prices due to amountOutMin = 0.\n\n## Recommended mitigation steps\n\nConsider redesigning UniswapPriceOracle to compute TWAP using two cumulative price observations from different timestamps (for example, store a prior observation in contract state and compute a windowed average). Consider enforcing a non-zero amountOutMin in BuyBackBurnerUniswap._performSwap() and keeping a post-swap price validation, so that manipulation must bypass multiple independent checks.\nDescribe the best method(s) to mitigate the finding.",
  "proof_of_concept": "// SPDX-License-Identifier: MIT\npragma solidity ^0.8.28;\n\nimport {Test} from \"forge-std/Test.sol\";\nimport {UniswapPriceOracle} from \"../contracts/oracles/UniswapPriceOracle.sol\";\n\n/// @title Mock Uniswap V2 Pair for PoC\n/// @dev Allows arbitrary reserve manipulation to simulate flash-loan attacks\ncontract MockUniswapV2Pair {\n    address public token0;\n    address public token1;\n\n    uint112 public reserve0;\n    uint112 public reserve1;\n    uint32 public blockTimestampLast;\n\n    uint256 public price0CumulativeLast;\n    uint256 public price1CumulativeLast;\n\n    constructor(address _token0, address _token1) {\n        token0 = _token0;\n        token1 = _token1;\n    }\n\n    function getReserves() external view returns (uint112, uint112, uint32) {\n        return (reserve0, reserve1, blockTimestampLast);\n    }\n\n    /// @dev Set reserves and timestamp to simulate pool state\n    function setReserves(uint112 _reserve0, uint112 _reserve1, uint32 _blockTimestampLast) external {\n        reserve0 = _reserve0;\n        reserve1 = _reserve1;\n        blockTimestampLast = _blockTimestampLast;\n    }\n\n    /// @dev Set cumulative prices to simulate historical TWAP accumulator state\n    function setCumulativePrices(uint256 _price0Cumulative, uint256 _price1Cumulative) external {\n        price0CumulativeLast = _price0Cumulative;\n        price1CumulativeLast = _price1Cumulative;\n    }\n}\n\n/// @title PoC: UniswapPriceOracle TWAP is algebraically broken\n/// @notice Demonstrates that validatePrice() always returns true because the\n///         \"TWAP\" computation reduces to the spot price, making slippage checks a no-op.\n///\n///         The vulnerable code (UniswapPriceOracle.sol lines 76-82):\n///           tradePrice = getPrice();                                              // spot price from current reserves\n///           cumulativePrice = cumulativePriceLast + (tradePrice * elapsedTime);   // fake cumulative\n///           timeWeightedAverage = (cumulativePrice - cumulativePriceLast) / elapsedTime;  // cancels out\n///\n///         Algebraically: timeWeightedAverage = (cPL + spot*dt - cPL) / dt = spot\n///         Therefore derivation = |spot - spot| / spot = 0, and the check always passes.\ncontract TestVulnOracleManipulation is Test {\n    MockUniswapV2Pair internal pair;\n    UniswapPriceOracle internal oracle;\n\n    address internal constant TOKEN_A = address(0xA); // \"native\" / second token\n    address internal constant TOKEN_B = address(0xB); // \"OLAS\"\n\n    function setUp() public {\n        // Deploy mock pair with token0 = TOKEN_A, token1 = TOKEN_B\n        pair = new MockUniswapV2Pair(TOKEN_A, TOKEN_B);\n\n        // Set initial \"fair\" reserves: 1000 TOKEN_A : 1000 TOKEN_B  => price = 1e18\n        // blockTimestampLast set to current block.timestamp so we can advance time later\n        pair.setReserves(1000e18, 1000e18, uint32(block.timestamp));\n\n        // Set some non-zero cumulative prices representing historical accumulation\n        // (the exact values do not matter for the bug, but we set realistic ones)\n        pair.setCumulativePrices(500_000e18, 500_000e18);\n\n        // Deploy oracle: direction = 0 means token0 is the \"native\" side\n        // _secondToken = TOKEN_A means if token0 == TOKEN_A, direction stays 0\n        // maxSlippage = 5 (representing 5% max slippage)\n        oracle = new UniswapPriceOracle(TOKEN_A, 5, address(pair));\n    }\n\n    /// @notice Demonstrates that after a 10x price manipulation, validatePrice() still returns true.\n    ///         In a correct TWAP implementation, a 10x deviation from historical average\n    ///         would cause the slippage check to fail.\n    function test_validatePrice_always_passes_after_manipulation() public {\n        // --- Step 1: Verify oracle works normally at fair price ---\n        uint256 fairPrice = oracle.getPrice();\n        // Fair price = (1000e18 * 1e18) / 1000e18 = 1e18\n        assertEq(fairPrice, 1e18, \"Fair price should be 1e18\");\n\n        // Advance time by 1 second so that elapsedTime > 0\n        vm.warp(block.timestamp + 1);\n\n        // validatePrice should pass at fair price\n        bool resultFair = oracle.validatePrice(5);\n        assertTrue(resultFair, \"validatePrice should pass at fair price\");\n\n        // --- Step 2: Simulate massive pool manipulation ---\n        // Attacker drains TOKEN_B (OLAS) from pool: 1000 TOKEN_A : 100 TOKEN_B\n        // This is a 10x price change: price goes from 1e18 to 0.1e18\n        // blockTimestampLast stays at the old timestamp to simulate that the\n        // manipulation happened via a swap (which updates reserves but the\n        // cumulative price was last updated at the old timestamp)\n        pair.setReserves(1000e18, 100e18, uint32(block.timestamp - 1));\n\n        // Advance time so elapsedTime > 0\n        vm.warp(block.timestamp + 1);\n\n        uint256 manipulatedPrice = oracle.getPrice();\n        // Manipulated price = (100e18 * 1e18) / 1000e18 = 0.1e18\n        assertEq(manipulatedPrice, 0.1e18, \"Manipulated price should be 0.1e18\");\n\n        // --- Step 3: Show that validatePrice STILL returns true despite 10x manipulation ---\n        // With a correct TWAP, a 10x deviation (900% change) would exceed any\n        // reasonable slippage parameter (e.g., 5%).\n        // But because TWAP == spot price due to the algebraic bug, derivation = 0.\n        bool resultManipulated = oracle.validatePrice(5);\n        assertTrue(resultManipulated, \"BUG: validatePrice passes even after 10x price manipulation\");\n    }\n\n    /// @notice Demonstrates the root cause: timeWeightedAverage always equals tradePrice.\n    ///         We test multiple extreme price scenarios to show derivation is always 0.\n    function test_twap_equals_spot_at_any_price() public {\n        // We test a range of manipulated reserve ratios\n        uint112[5] memory reserveA = [uint112(1000e18), uint112(1000e18), uint112(100e18), uint112(1e18), uint112(10000e18)];\n        uint112[5] memory reserveB = [uint112(1000e18), uint112(1e18),    uint112(1000e18), uint112(1000e18), uint112(1e18)];\n        // Corresponding prices:       1.0e18,           0.001e18,          10e18,           1000e18,           0.0001e18\n\n        for (uint256 i = 0; i < 5; i++) {\n            // Set arbitrary reserves\n            pair.setReserves(reserveA[i], reserveB[i], uint32(block.timestamp));\n\n            // Advance time\n            vm.warp(block.timestamp + 10);\n\n            // validatePrice should FAIL for extreme deviations from the original 1:1 ratio\n            // but it ALWAYS passes because TWAP == spot price\n            bool result = oracle.validatePrice(5);\n            assertTrue(result, \"BUG: validatePrice passes at every price ratio, slippage check is a no-op\");\n        }\n    }\n\n    /// @notice Shows that validatePrice returns false ONLY when block.timestamp == blockTimestampLast\n    ///         (same block), which is the only \"protection\" -- but attackers simply wait one block.\n    function test_only_fails_in_same_block() public {\n        // Set blockTimestampLast to current timestamp (same block)\n        pair.setReserves(1000e18, 1000e18, uint32(block.timestamp));\n\n        // In the same block, validatePrice returns false (the only guard)\n        bool resultSameBlock = oracle.validatePrice(5);\n        assertFalse(resultSameBlock, \"Should return false in same block\");\n\n        // Advance by just 1 second -- now it passes regardless of reserves\n        vm.warp(block.timestamp + 1);\n        pair.setReserves(1000e18, 1e18, uint32(block.timestamp - 1)); // 1000x manipulation\n\n        bool resultNextBlock = oracle.validatePrice(5);\n        assertTrue(resultNextBlock, \"BUG: 1000x manipulation passes after just 1 second\");\n    }\n\n    /// @notice Mathematical proof: reproduce the exact computation from validatePrice()\n    ///         and show that timeWeightedAverage == tradePrice for ANY inputs.\n    function test_algebraic_proof() public {\n        // Set up some state\n        pair.setReserves(1000e18, 500e18, uint32(block.timestamp));\n        pair.setCumulativePrices(123456789e18, 987654321e18);\n\n        vm.warp(block.timestamp + 100); // elapsedTime = 100\n\n        // Reproduce the oracle's computation manually\n        uint256 cumulativePriceLast = pair.price1CumulativeLast(); // direction=0 => price1CumulativeLast\n        uint256 elapsedTime = block.timestamp - (block.timestamp - 100); // = 100\n\n        uint256 tradePrice = oracle.getPrice(); // spot price from current reserves\n        // tradePrice = (500e18 * 1e18) / 1000e18 = 0.5e18\n\n        // The oracle's \"TWAP\" computation:\n        uint256 cumulativePrice = cumulativePriceLast + (tradePrice * elapsedTime);\n        uint256 timeWeightedAverage = (cumulativePrice - cumulativePriceLast) / elapsedTime;\n\n        // PROOF: timeWeightedAverage == tradePrice (always)\n        // (cPL + spot*dt - cPL) / dt = spot*dt / dt = spot\n        assertEq(timeWeightedAverage, tradePrice, \"PROOF: TWAP always equals spot price\");\n\n        // Therefore derivation = 0\n        uint256 derivation = (tradePrice > timeWeightedAverage)\n            ? ((tradePrice - timeWeightedAverage) * 1e16) / timeWeightedAverage\n            : ((timeWeightedAverage - tradePrice) * 1e16) / timeWeightedAverage;\n\n        assertEq(derivation, 0, \"PROOF: derivation is always zero\");\n    }\n}",
